---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const recycleCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: '100/(1 + 4*x)', label: 'reactant leaving unreacted', color: '#b45309', width: 2.4 },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 5, label: 'number of passes through the reactor' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 105, label: '% of feed still unconverted' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 330,
};

const balancesCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Draw the box', note: 'Pick a boundary around the process — one unit or the whole plant. Everything outside is the environment.' },
    { math: '2 · Mass in − mass out = accumulation', note: 'The conservation law, written for the box. Steady state means accumulation is zero: whatever flows in flows out.' },
    { math: '3 · Account for reactions', note: 'For reacting systems, balance atoms, not molecules: 4 Fe + 3 O₂ → 2 Fe₂O₃ conserves each element even as compounds vanish.' },
    { math: '4 · Energy gets the same treatment', note: 'Energy in − energy out = accumulation, with enthalpy carrying the chemistry. A reactor that consumes heat must be fed it.' },
    { math: '5 · Recycle changes the arithmetic', note: 'Recycling unreacted feed raises overall conversion without a better reactor — but purge streams are needed to keep inerts from accumulating.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/chemical-engineering/foundational/material-and-energy-balances">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Chemical engineering is chemistry with consequences at scale — and its foundational tool is
    disarmingly simple: draw a box around the process and account for what crosses it. Mass and
    energy are conserved, so every kilogram and every joule can be followed. Nearly all process
    design starts as an exercise in bookkeeping.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the balance equation, from box to recycle" widget="equationStep" config={balancesCfg} />

  <h2>Why recycle beats bigger reactors</h2>
  <p>
    Real reactions never go to completion in one pass. Sending unreacted feed back around turns one
    low conversion into many high ones: after n passes, the unconverted fraction falls like 1/(1+R)
    for recycle ratio R. The trade-off is pumping cost and purge design — recycle accumulates
    anything that does not react.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="unconverted feed falls with each pass" widget="plotter" config={recycleCfg} />

  <h2>Energy is the other ledger</h2>
  <p>
    Every unit operation is also an energy balance. Distillation columns boil and condense tonnes
    of liquid; exothermic reactors must be cooled or they run away. The engineer's first questions
    — where does the heat come from, where does it go, can it be reused — are energy balances with
    a heat exchanger catalogue open.
  </p>

  <h2>From balances to flowsheets</h2>
  <p>
    A full process is hundreds of coupled balances, solved on a flowsheet simulator. But the
    discipline's instinct stays the same as the back-of-envelope version: pick the box, count what
    crosses it, and trust the conservation laws. When the numbers refuse to close, it is usually
    the flowsheet, not the physics, that is wrong.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NCEES FE Chemical exam specifications; Felder &amp; Rousseau, <em>Elementary Principles of Chemical Processes</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
